# Who must approve a change to this repository, and why.
#
# This repository publishes what OSS Verify asserts: the theorem statements, the binary each statement is about, and
# the table that says which statements are proved.  One rule decides every line below.
#
#   A person approves a change to what we ASSERT, or to what DECIDES whether an assertion is checked.
#   A machine catches everything else, and reads every line rather than a path name.
#
# The owners are @sjawhar and @JasonGross, the two people who own /theories/ in the internal repository.  The ruleset
# of main requires a code owner's approval and no other approval, so a change that touches none of the paths below
# needs nobody, and a change that touches one of them needs one of those two.
#
# tools/oft/paths.py already sorts every path in this repository into hand, facts, gen and contrib, and the lines
# below follow it rather than a second opinion: every `facts` row is owned, every `contrib` row is not.
#
# WHAT WE ASSERT.  Owned.
#
#   Statements.v          The theorems as we publish them.
#   /theories/            Driver.v, Images.v, Premises.v, Shapes.v: the definitions every Statements.v is written
#                         against.  A change here changes what every statement means without touching a statement.
#   harness.yaml          harness.yaml pins by SHA-256 the ELF file each statement is about, and it is also what
#   project.yaml          Statements.v is rendered from; project.yaml names the library and the commit it was built
#   /release.yaml         from; release.yaml pins the semantics, the Rocq version and the checker and runner images.
#                         A swapped binary makes a true statement false without touching the statement, so the file
#                         that chooses the binary is owned exactly as the statement is.
#   Distribution.v        Not generated, whatever its header looks like: paths.py calls its body a fact, and
#                         generate.py keeps the body it finds and re-renders only the header.  Every
#                         `disagrees_at_most` statement is measured against Distribution.w and Distribution.M, so
#                         editing this file changes what a bound statement claims while Statements.v stands still.
#   symbols/              A build's symbol table, read from the unstripped twin of the pinned binary.  Symbols.v and
#                         the function names a statement talks about come from it.  Four files today, the four
#                         builds of libreac/fuzz_pcap.
#   source/               The harness source we publish as the thing that was built.
#   /index/               The published tables: statements.tsv, proofs.tsv, binaries.tsv, harnesses.tsv, projects.tsv
#                         and their two rendered pages.  `oft generate` writes them and CI holds them equal to their
#                         rendering, so they cannot drift on their own; they are owned because the row that says open
#                         or proved is the claim a reader reads.  This costs a prover nothing: the six proof pull
#                         requests open when this file was written (#19 to #24) change 412, 282 and similar numbers
#                         of files between them and not one file under index/, because the shared tables are
#                         rendered again after a merge and when a release is cut.
#   /NOTICE               What we tell the world we owe to whom.
#   findings/,            What we say is wrong with somebody else's library, and the patch text we would send them.
#   patches/,             Sami, 2026-11-07: "we need to have an extremely high bar for sending bug reports and
#   unpatched.yaml        patches upstream ... so that every bug report we send improves our reputation"
#                         (dispatch://OSSV-286/comment/fe1d6fb9).  unpatched.yaml is also the switch that releases a
#                         held project's content into this public repository.
#                         Those five patterns match no file of the published tree today; the only paths under them
#                         are fixtures below tools/oft/tests/, which /tools/ owns anyway.  They are here so that the
#                         first real one arrives through a person, instead of our finding the gap afterwards.  The
#                         .patch files that DO exist, under proofs/provenance/recipe/patches/, are a build record
#                         and stay unowned: these patterns are anchored so they cannot reach them.
#
# WHAT DECIDES WHETHER CHECKING HAPPENS.  Owned.
#
#   /.github/             The workflows, the actions they call and actionlint's configuration.  No carve-out: that
#                         includes .github/held/, whose files pin the detectors that keep held content off this
#                         public repository.
#   /tools/, /oft,        The tools those workflows run: `oft lint`, `oft generate --check`, `oft check`.  ./theorem
#   /theorem              is a one-line shell script that executes ./oft.
#   /toolchain/           allowed-axioms.txt, which decides which axioms a proof may use, and the checker and runner
#                         images' Dockerfiles.  A proof is only as good as this list.
#   /tests/               The positive controls that must pass and the negative controls that must be refused
#                         (tests/check.sh, tests/negative/, tests/mutate.py).  A change here can leave every other
#                         check passing over nothing.
#   /.claude/, AGENTS*.md What an agent loads in a job that holds a token.  CLAUDE*.md and /.mcp.json match no file
#   CLAUDE*.md,           in this repository today; they are listed for the same reason as findings/ above.
#   /.mcp.json
#   /.gitattributes       A path marked -diff is not shown in review.
#   /.github/CODEOWNERS   This file, which says who owns the rest.
#
# WHAT THE MACHINE OWNS, AND NOBODY SIGNS.  Deliberately absent from the list below.
#
#   Proof bodies -- everything under projects/<project>/<target>/proofs/ -- have no owner.  The Rocq kernel is their
#   reviewer: a proof checks against the statement or it does not, and a human reading adds nothing to that verdict.
#   Sami ruled this on 2026-11-07: "It is correct that the proof body needs no Human Reviewer"
#   (dispatch://OSSV-286/comment/c7500b0c-e76c-4cb0-bf86-9dea75718d29).
#
#   The same holds for everything else a machine can check line by line, and for the same reason -- a path name is a
#   worse reader than the check that reads the content.  These are the `gen` and `contrib` rows of paths.py:
#     README.md at every level, Images.v, Symbols.v, Characterise.v, the dune files and dune-project are written by
#       `oft generate` from the owned facts, and `oft generate --check` fails if they differ;
#     projects/<project>/<target>/counterexamples/ is replayed against the pinned binary;
#     projects/<project>/<target>/proofs/provenance/ is a build record, regenerated by made_from.py --check.  Note
#       that provenance/port/projects/<project>/ carries an archived copy of a project.yaml: the project.yaml line
#       below is anchored to /projects/*/project.yaml so that those 29 copies stay unowned, which is why it is not
#       written as a bare file name.
#   docs/ is prose about the claims and is not the claim; theories/ and Statements.v are.
#
# The last pattern that matches a file decides its owner, so these lines stay the last ones in this file.
/release.yaml @sjawhar @JasonGross
/NOTICE @sjawhar @JasonGross
/theories/ @sjawhar @JasonGross
